      "hints": [
        "Combine fractions on LHS over $(1 - \\sin\\theta)(1 + \\sin\\theta) = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta$.",
        "Expand numerator to $2\\sin^2\\theta$ and show $\\frac{2\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta} = 2\\tan^2\\theta$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Put both algebraic fractions on the LHS over their common denominator:\n$$\\text{LHS} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta(1 + \\sin\\theta) - \\sin\\theta(1 - \\sin\\theta)}{(1 - \\sin\\theta)(1 + \\sin\\theta)}$$",
        "Expand and simplify the numerator:\n$$\\text{Numerator} = \\sin\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta - \\sin\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 2\\sin^2\\theta$$",
        "Simplify the denominator using $1 - \\sin^2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta$:\n$$\\text{LHS} = \\frac{2\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta} = 2\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)^2 = 2\\tan^2\\theta$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
